==== exit ====
0
==== stdout ====
raphael - an actionable second brain for agents

Usage: raphael <command> [options]

Start here:
  1. Put RAPHAEL_API_KEY=local-development-key-change-this in a private .env file in the current directory.
  2. Run raphael server serve; keep that terminal open.
  3. In another terminal run raphael login. Enter http://127.0.0.1:3000 and the same key.
  4. Run raphael list /, then raphael create resource.note /work/first-note --body 'My first note'.
  Already have a server? Start at step 3 with its address and key. See raphael help connecting.

Read:
  favorites          List favorites by title.
  get                Read one item and its current revision.
  path               Print an item’s current full path.
  list               List children of one or more paths or ids.
  search             Search all descendants by text.
  help               Read command help and cross-command guidance.

Write:
  create             Create an area, project, or note.
  update             Change an item with revision protection.
  move               Move an item while keeping its id.
  archive            Hide an item and everything inside it.
  restore            Remove an item’s own archive cause.
  favorite           Add an area or project to favorites.
  unfavorite         Remove an area or project from favorites.

Setup:
  login              Save a verified server address and API key on this machine.
  server             Manage the server on this machine.
  server serve       Run a Raphael server on this machine.
  version            Print the installed Raphael version.

What things are:
  An area holds ongoing work. A project holds work with an outcome. A note holds content.
  Types: area, project, resource.note. Items that can hold children: area, project.
  The root (/) can directly hold area.
  area can directly hold area, project, resource.note.
  project can directly hold resource.note.
  resource.note cannot hold children.
  Address an item by absolute path or stable id; a revision protects a write from concurrent edits.

Topics:
  addressing         Paths, ids, slugs, containment and field limits.
  content            Note bodies, files, stdin and Markdown conversion.
  revisions          Safe writes, conflicts, archives and uncertain outcomes.
  output             Text results, JSON envelopes, paging and diagnostics.
  connecting         Start a local server and connect a client.
  exit-codes         Choose recovery from the process exit code.
  reference          All command pages and topics in one stream.
  Use raphael help <command-or-topic>, or raphael <command> --help.
  raphael help --json prints machine-readable definitions.

Examples:
  raphael login
    Connect using the server address and key.
  raphael create resource.note /work/first-note --body 'My first note'
    Write a note in the initial work area.
  raphael list /
    Read the top level.

Options:
  -h, --help    Show help.
      --version Show the version.

Exit codes:
  0    Done. This includes no-ops and success with warnings.
  1    Failed for another reason, with nothing left uncertain.
  2    The command as typed can never succeed; nothing was applied.
  3    Not found.
  4    Conflicts with stored state; nothing was applied.
  5    Not configured, or not authorized.
  6    Not applied, and retrying may work.
  7    Outcome unknown: a write was sent and no definite answer came back.
  130  Interrupted by SIGINT.
  143  Interrupted by SIGTERM.

---

Usage: raphael create <type> <path> [options]
       raphael create <type> --parent-id <id> --slug <slug> [options]

Create an area, project, or note.

Types: area, project, resource.note. A full path supplies both parent and slug. The parent must already exist.

Areas and projects require --title. Notes may derive their title from the first body line, then the description. Metadata is set once, at create.

Rules:
  See help addressing for containment and slug rules. A taken slug, including one reserved by an archived item, is refused.
  When supplying a body, give either --body or --body-literal. Give each tag its own --tag.

Options:
      --title <text>
      Name, at most 200 characters.
      --parent-id <id>
      Parent id instead of a full path. Requires --slug.
      --slug <slug>
      Address within the parent, at most 100 characters. See help addressing.
      --description <text>
      Description, at most 4000 characters. An empty string clears it.
      --tag <tag>
      Repeat for each tag; at most 25 tags of 50 characters. Commas are refused. Updates replace all tags. Repeatable.
      --metadata <json|@file>
      JSON object set once at create. @- is refused.
      --body <text|@file|@->
      Body text, @file, or @- for stdin. An empty string clears it; updates require --revision.
      --body-literal <text>
      Literal body text, including text beginning with @. Updates require --revision.
      --body-format <format>
      Submitted body: markdown or tiptap. Default: markdown.
      --return-format <format>
      Returned body: markdown or tiptap. Requires --json. Default: markdown.
      --json
      Print one JSON result.
  -h, --help
      Show this help.

Examples:
  raphael create area /work --title Work
  raphael create project /work/raphael --title Raphael
  raphael create resource.note /work/raphael/api-design --body '# API design'
  raphael create resource.note --parent-id 42 --slug review --title Review --tag reviewed

Prints:
  The created item with its id and revision. JSON carries `entity`.

Next: raphael list / -r

See also: raphael help addressing; raphael help content; raphael help update

---

Usage: raphael update <path> [changes] [options]
       raphael update --id <id> [changes] [options]

Change an item with revision protection.

Give at least one change. Tags replace the complete tag list; --no-tags clears it. A renamed title does not change the path.

A body replacement requires the revision you read. Other changes may read and pin the current id and revision for you. Conflicts are never retried automatically.

Rules:
  Metadata is set once, at create. --active and --inactive apply only to projects.
  On a revision conflict, get the item again and reconcile your intended change before updating with its new revision.

Options:
      --id <id>
      Address by durable id instead of path.
      --title <text>
      Name, at most 200 characters.
      --description <text>
      Description, at most 4000 characters. An empty string clears it.
      --slug <slug>
      Address within the parent, at most 100 characters. See help addressing.
      --body <text|@file|@->
      Body text, @file, or @- for stdin. An empty string clears it; updates require --revision.
      --body-literal <text>
      Literal body text, including text beginning with @. Updates require --revision.
      --body-format <format>
      Submitted body: markdown or tiptap. Default: markdown.
      --tag <tag>
      Repeat for each tag; at most 25 tags of 50 characters. Commas are refused. Updates replace all tags. Repeatable.
      --no-tags
      Clear all tags.
      --active
      Mark the project active.
      --inactive
      Mark the project inactive.
      --revision <n>
      Revision read previously. Without a body change, omitted revisions are read immediately before sending.
      --return-format <format>
      Returned body: markdown or tiptap. Requires --json. Default: markdown.
      --json
      Print one JSON result.
  -h, --help
      Show this help.

Examples:
  raphael update /work/raphael/api-design --body '# Revised design' --revision 8
  raphael update /work/raphael --tag reviewed --tag contracts
  raphael update --id 42 --title 'Contract review' --description ''

Prints:
  The updated item and revision. JSON carries `entity`.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help revisions; raphael help content

