Usage: raphael archive <path> [options]
       raphael archive --id <id> [options]

Hide an item and everything inside it.

Archive hides items from ordinary lists and search without deleting them. The slug stays reserved, and favorites and project active state are retained.

Archiving an item already hidden through a parent adds its own archive cause. Restoring the parent then leaves this item archived. There is no delete command.

Options:
      --id <id>
      Address by durable id instead of path.
      --revision <n>
      Revision read previously. Without a body change, omitted revisions are read immediately before sending.
      --json
      Print one JSON result.
  -h, --help
      Show this help.

Examples:
  raphael archive /work/raphael
  raphael archive --id 42
  raphael archive --id 42 --revision 8 --json

Prints:
  The item and remaining archive causes. JSON carries `node` and `archiveCauses`.

Next: raphael restore --id 42

See also: raphael help restore; raphael help revisions

---

Usage: raphael restore <path> [options]
       raphael restore --id <id> [options]

Remove an item’s own archive cause.

Restore removes only the item’s own archive cause. If a parent remains archived, the item stays archived and the answer names the parent to restore.

An already active item succeeds without a change.

Options:
      --id <id>
      Address by durable id instead of path.
      --revision <n>
      Revision read previously. Without a body change, omitted revisions are read immediately before sending.
      --json
      Print one JSON result.
  -h, --help
      Show this help.

Examples:
  raphael restore /work/raphael
  raphael restore --id 42
  raphael restore --id 42 --revision 8 --json

Prints:
  The item and remaining archive causes. JSON carries `node` and `archiveCauses`.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help archive; raphael help revisions

